OK, so `arith_uncompress_O0_RLE` and `arith_uncompress_O1_RLE` also don't free anything on error. They just return NULL.

Let me step back and think about this differently. The crash is a heap-double-free in `decode_names`. The `free_context` function frees all `desc[i].buf` for `i < max_tok*16`. The double-free happens when two `desc[i].buf` pointers are the same.

Let me look at the copy path in `decode_names` once more:

```c
if (ttype & 64) {
    if (o+2 >= sz) goto err;
    int j = in[o++]<<4;
    j += in[o++];
    if (ttype & 128) {
        tnum++;
        if (tnum >= MAX_TOKENS)
            goto err;
        ctx->max_tok = tnum+1;
        memset(&ctx->desc[tnum<<4], 0, 16*sizeof(ctx->desc[tnum]));
    }

    if ((ttype & 15) != 0 && (ttype & 128)) {
        ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf = malloc(nreads);
        if (!ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf)
            goto err;

        ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf_l = 0;
        ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf_a = nreads;
        ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf[0] = ttype&15;
        memset(&ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf[1], N_MATCH, nreads-1);
    }

    i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15);
    if (j >= i)
        goto err;

    ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
    ctx->desc[i].buf_a = ctx->desc[j].buf_a;
    if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
    ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
    if (!ctx->desc[i].buf)
        goto err;

    memcpy(ctx->desc[i].buf, ctx->desc[j].buf, ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
    continue;
}
```

Wait, I need to look at this more carefully. The `memset(&ctx->desc[tnum<<4], 0, 16*sizeof(ctx->desc[tnum]))` zeroes out `desc[tnum<<4]` through `desc[tnum<<4+15]`. This means any previously allocated buffers in those slots are leaked (not freed), but not double-freed.

But here's the key: `ctx->max_tok = tnum+1`. So `free_context` will free `desc[0]` through `desc[(tnum+1)*16 - 1]`. If `tnum` is incremented multiple times, `max_tok` grows. But the `memset` zeroes out the new slots, so they start as NULL.

Hmm, but what if `tnum` is incremented, then later a copy operation references `desc[j]` where `j` is in a previously-zeroed range? The `desc[j].buf` would be NULL, and `memcpy` with NULL source would crash (not double-free).

Wait, let me reconsider. What if `ttype&64` is set but `ttype&128` is NOT set? Then `tnum` is not incremented, and `desc[tnum<<4]` is not zeroed. So `desc[tnum<<4]` through `desc[tnum<<4+15]` retain their previous values.

Now, `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15)`. And `j < i`. The copy operation:
```c
ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
ctx->desc[i].buf_a = ctx->desc[j].buf_a;
if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
if (!ctx->desc[i].buf)
    goto err;
memcpy(ctx->desc[i].buf, ctx->desc[j].buf, ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
```

This frees `desc[i].buf` and allocates a new one. Still no aliasing.

